Subject: wasix: keep clock offsets in atomics instead of a locked map

The offsets a guest can put on its clocks were held in a
Mutex<HashMap<Snapshot0Clockid, i64>> on the WASI state. That is the
wrong shape for what the offsets are: there are four of them, one per
clock the enum names that a guest can adjust, they are written only by
clock_time_set, which almost no guest ever calls, and they are read by
clock_time_get, which a guest calls constantly, a guest that stamps its
work being the worst case. So a reading of the clock took a mutex and
hashed a clock id to find, almost always, nothing. In a profile of a
guest under load, clock_time_get was 4% of the runtime's user-mode
samples on top of the checks every syscall makes.

Hold the offsets in a fixed array of four atomics instead, one slot per
clock that can carry one, reached only through clock_offset and
set_clock_offset. Reading a clock is now one relaxed load added to the
platform reading, with zero, the value every slot starts at, meaning
the guest has not moved that clock; setting one is a relaxed store.
Relaxed is enough on both sides because an offset is a value in its own
right and orders nothing else: a reading taken while another thread is
setting that clock may be taken either side of the set, exactly as one
taken an instant earlier would have been. The unknown clock id has no
slot, since reading or setting it fails in the platform call before an
offset is consulted or stored, and neither does any id the enum may
gain. The fork and the init duplicate copy the four values, as they
copied the map.

Unit tests cover an offset being read back and replaced, a clock the
guest has not set carrying none of another clock's offset, a clock that
cannot carry an offset reading as the host reports it, and a forked
state and a duplicated init carrying the offsets over.

